The density of alkaline earth metals varies in the following order:-

\mathrm{Ca} < \mathrm{Mg} < \mathrm{Be} < \mathrm{Sr} < \mathrm{Ba} < \mathrm{Ra}

This is due to low mass and high volume of $\mathrm{Ca}$ compared to other members.

( Alkali metals are strong reducing agents because after the removal of the only valence electron $\left(n s^1\right)$ they can gain the electronic configuration of noble gases $(n-1) p^6$, that is more stable electronic configuration.
$\mathrm{KO}_2$ is paramagnetic oxide due to the presence of 1 unpaired electrons. The number of electrons present is 17 where the sixteen electrons are from two oxygen atoms and one from the negative charge $\left(\mathrm{O}_2^{-}\right)$. Its molecular orbital configuration can be written as

\begin{aligned}
& \sigma 1 s^2 \sigma * 1 s^2 \sigma 2 s^2 \sigma * 2 s^2 \sigma 2 p_z^2 \pi 2 p_x^2=\pi 2 p_y^2 \pi * 2 p_x^2 \\
&=\pi * 2 p_y^1 \sigma * 2 p_z^0 .
\end{aligned}

So, statement (B) is worng.
The hydration enthalpies of alkali metal ions decrease with increase in ionic sizes.

\mathrm{Li}^{+}>\mathrm{Na}^{+}>\mathrm{K}^{+}>\mathrm{Rb}^{+}

$\mathrm{Li}^{+}$has maximum degree of hydration and for this reason, lithium salts are mostly hydrated, e.g. $\mathrm{LiCl} \cdot 2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}$.

Halides of alkaline earth metals form hydrate, so they are hygroscopic in nature. Due to this nature, they can be used as water absorbing
compound.
$\mathrm{Mg}$ is smaller than $\mathrm{Ca}$. Hence, $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ ion polarises water molecule easily than the calcium water molecules get attached to $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ as the water of crystallisation.
Similarly $\mathrm{Sr}$ is smaller than $\mathrm{Ba}$ and easily polarises by water molecule.

\begin{aligned}
& \text { i.e. } \mathrm{MgCl}_2 \cdot 6 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}>\mathrm{CaCl}_2 \cdot 6 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \text {, } \\
& \mathrm{SrCl}_2 \cdot 2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}>\mathrm{BaCl}_2 \cdot 2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}
\end{aligned}

Hence, correct order is $\mathrm{Mg}\gt \mathrm{Ca}$ and $\mathrm{Sr}\gt \mathrm{Ba}$.

